Operator Guidance: Coupled Mathematical–Architectural Advancement and Required Safeguards

To all models currently working on this project:

The mathematics and the research architecture are not separate workstreams. They are a coupled system that must advance together.

The mathematical program supplies the difficult, adversarial environment needed to expose weaknesses in the architecture. The architecture, in turn, forces the mathematics to identify its exact objects, probability laws, coordinate systems, regimes, dependencies, evidence classes, and limits of applicability.

As one side advances, the other must be checked for corresponding advancement or demonstrated sufficiency.

This reciprocal relationship is intended to become one of the program's strongest safeguards. However, it creates several risks that must now be addressed explicitly.

1. Coupled Advancement Invariant

For every material mathematical transition from one research state to another, the record must show one of the following:

1. A corresponding architectural change that addresses a newly exposed requirement, ambiguity, failure mode, or coordination burden; or
2. A formal no-change certificate demonstrating that the existing architecture already represented, tested, and governed the mathematical transition correctly.

Silence does not count as evidence that the architecture kept pace.

A no-change certificate must state, at minimum:

* The exact mathematical object that changed status.
* The mathematical statement established, modified, killed, or superseded.
* The evidence class supporting the transition.
* The object card, law, regime, normalization, and coordinate system involved.
* The dependencies affected.
* The dependencies explicitly unaffected.
* The existing architectural controls that already covered the transition.
* Why no new schema, verifier rule, register field, status class, or governance mechanism is needed.
* What failure would reveal that the no-change judgment was incorrect.

The converse rule also applies:

Every material architectural change must cite a concrete mathematical failure mode, coordination failure, provenance defect, recurring ambiguity, measurable resource leak, or anticipated adversarial risk that it is designed to address.

Architecture must not grow merely because additional structure is possible.

2. Unexplained Advancement Is an Alarm Condition

The following conditions must be treated as diagnostic alarms:

A. Mathematics advances while the architecture remains unchanged without explanation

This may indicate:

* An untracked assumption.
* A newly introduced object class.
* A measure or conditioning mismatch.
* A coordinate or normalization dependence.
* A planar-versus-torus conflation.
* A limiting-versus-finite-scale conflation.
* A local-to-global inference that is not represented.
* A new dependency not reflected in the machine or ledger.
* A verifier that no longer tests the exact promoted statement.

Do not assume that a mathematically persuasive derivation automatically fits the existing architecture.

B. Architecture expands while mathematical uncertainty does not shrink

This may indicate:

* Bureaucratic overgrowth.
* Multiple registers encoding the same state.
* Review artifacts reviewing other review artifacts without reducing uncertainty.
* Governance work displacing proof work.
* Status proliferation without improved discrimination.
* Repeated coordination that does not close, kill, falsify, or sharpen an item.

Architectural growth must be justified by measurable reduction in error risk, ambiguity, duplicated work, human review load, or unresolved uncertainty.

3. Do Not Allow Both Sides to Share the Same Hidden Error

Mathematics and architecture can evolve together and still be wrong if both inherit the same mistaken assumption.

A rigorous proof of the wrong object, perfectly tracked by a rigorous system, remains a failure.

This is the synchronized-calibration risk.

The project therefore requires periodic challenges from outside the active internal reasoning ecology. Appropriate methods include:

* Blind reconstruction from primitive definitions.
* A model or reviewer that has not read the existing derivation lineage.
* Independently generated notation and coordinate choices.
* Alternative formalizations of the original verbal research objective.
* Planted negative controls and deliberately mutated fixtures.
* Attempts to prove that the formally defined object does not answer the intended scientific question.
* External specialist review when the work becomes mature enough.
* Literature comparison after internally generated results are frozen, not as a substitute for derivation.
* Gemini or other observer models being used as independent eyes and ears when activated by Dylan.

An outside reviewer should not merely be asked whether the internal proof is correct. The reviewer should also be asked:

Is this the correct mathematical object for the original research objective?

4. Guard Against False Independence

Multiple model lines agreeing does not automatically constitute independent evidence.

Model lines may share:

* Training data.
* Architecture.
* Prompts.
* Upstream derivations.
* Copied notation.
* The same hidden assumptions.
* The same computational fixtures.
* Summaries written by another model rather than original source material.

Every claimed independent review must disclose:

* Which source artifacts were read.
* Whether the original derivation was visible.
* Whether the reviewer reconstructed the result independently.
* Whether the same code, symbolic notebook, or numerical fixture was reused.
* Which assumptions were inherited.
* What genuinely independent route was used.
* Whether the review checked the object definition or only the internal algebra.

Agreement should be weighted by methodological independence, not model count alone.

5. Guard Against Consensus Laundering

A statement must not become more authoritative merely because it has been repeatedly summarized, cited, copied into registers, or described as accepted.

Every promotion path must remain traceable to primary evidence.

Models must watch for the following pattern:

1. A candidate claim appears.
2. Another artifact summarizes it.
3. Later models read the summary instead of the derivation.
4. The summary is cited as evidence of prior acceptance.
5. Repetition gradually becomes mistaken for proof.

To prevent this:

* Primary derivations and executable receipts must remain identifiable.
* Summaries must not be treated as independent verification.
* Ballots govern project action; they do not establish mathematical truth.
* A majority vote cannot repair a missing proof.
* A closure record must cite the exact evidence that supports closure.
* Any reviewer unable to inspect the primary evidence must record that limitation.

6. Guard Against Closure Inflation

The easy-closure-first rule is intended to retire genuinely settled items. It must not be used to make broad claims appear settled by closing narrow components.

Every closure package must state:

* The exact object closed.
* The exact statement closed.
* The regime in which it is closed.
* The coordinate and normalization dependence.
* Whether the result is exact, asymptotic, numerical, conditional, or instrument-level.
* What broader claims remain open.
* Which tempting extrapolations are forbidden.
* What evidence would reopen the item.

Examples of required distinctions include:

* Canonical local normal form versus physical-field coefficient.
* Planar coalesced law versus exact finite-torus law.
* Instrument verification versus theorem verification.
* Typed pair-Palm probability versus adjacency-conditioned probability.
* Exact finite-r statement versus limiting jet statement.
* Graph persistence versus continuum persistence.
* Candidate theorem versus canonical theorem.

A narrow closure must reduce active uncertainty without silently widening its own scope.

7. Guard Against Local Rigor and Global Drift

It is possible for every local calculation to be correct while the overall research program proves the wrong statement.

Therefore, each significant proof chain must periodically undergo a global-object audit addressing:

* What original scientific question is this chain supposed to answer?
* What exact theorem would answer that question?
* Is the current chain proving that theorem or a nearby surrogate?
* Has the probability measure changed?
* Has conditioning changed?
* Has the pairing rule changed?
* Has the domain changed?
* Has a limiting object replaced a finite object?
* Has a coordinate-dependent quantity been treated as invariant?
* Has a computationally convenient proxy replaced the target?
* Are the active machine roots still aligned with the current theorem statement?

A long chain of correct lemmas does not compensate for target drift.

8. Treat Mathematical Errors as Architectural Test Cases

When a meaningful mathematical error is found, do not stop after correcting the derivation.

Determine whether the failure reveals a reusable architectural weakness.

For every serious error, ask:

* What allowed this error to survive?
* Was the object insufficiently typed?
* Was the evidence class unclear?
* Did a verifier test the wrong estimand?
* Did duplicate files appear independent?
* Did a summary outrun its source?
* Was a local statement promoted globally?
* Did the machine state fail to track a theorem revision?
* Was there no negative control capable of detecting the mistake?
* Did the review process inherit the same assumption?

Where justified, convert the failure into one or more of the following:

* A schema field.
* An object-card distinction.
* A verifier rule.
* A negative test.
* A preregistration requirement.
* A closure restriction.
* A provenance rule.
* A machine-state constraint.
* A named failure class.
* A future reviewer instruction.

The goal is not to create architecture after every typo. The goal is to prevent the recurrence of structural error classes.

9. Treat Architectural Failures as Mathematical Risks

Architecture defects can contaminate mathematical conclusions.

Examples include:

* Duplicate artifacts counting as independent evidence.
* Identifier collisions merging unrelated derivations.
* Stale summaries overriding corrected sources.
* Partial automation refreshes appearing current.
* Unreadable file types silently disappearing from the register.
* Model-written instructions inside indexed documents changing system behavior.
* A candidate result appearing in canonical navigation.
* A closure record removing an item before its dependency effects are mapped.

These are not merely administrative inconveniences. They alter what evidence models see and therefore alter the mathematics models produce.

Architecture incidents must be assessed for downstream mathematical contamination.

10. Required Transition Record

For each material mathematical or architectural advance, create or update a transition record containing:

1. Previous state
2. New state
3. Exact object
4. Exact claim or architectural rule
5. Evidence added
6. Evidence removed, killed, or superseded
7. Independent review status
8. Dependencies affected
9. Dependencies unaffected
10. Mathematical change
11. Architectural change
12. No-change certificate, where applicable
13. New failure modes introduced
14. Tests capable of detecting those failures
15. Uncertainty permanently removed
16. Remaining uncertainty
17. Required human decision
18. Reopening condition

This record should be concise enough to use consistently but complete enough to prevent silent drift.

11. Measure Whether the Architecture Is Helping

The system should not evaluate itself by artifact count, model activity, or review volume.

Useful indicators include:

* Number of active uncertainties permanently retired.
* Number of recurring error classes converted into tests.
* Reduction in duplicate model work.
* Reduction in stale or contradictory status claims.
* Time from discovery of an easy item to terminal closure.
* Number of claims independently reconstructed from primary definitions.
* Number of attempted promotions blocked because of object mismatch.
* Number of architectural changes with a demonstrated failure mode.
* Ratio of mathematical advances to coordination artifacts.
* Number of reopened closures and why they failed.
* Human-review burden per settled result.
* Percentage of active claims with complete object cards and dependency maps.

An increase in coordination activity without a reduction in uncertainty is not progress.

12. Preserve the Role of External Observers

Gemini and any other observer model activated by Dylan should not automatically be absorbed into the active internal consensus process.

Their value may be greatest when they retain distance from the shared derivation lineage.

Observer tasks may include:

* Reviewing the original research objective without reading internal conclusions.
* Identifying assumptions the active models all share.
* Comparing the formal object against the intended scientific meaning.
* Finding omitted alternatives.
* Challenging whether the architecture itself is producing path dependence.
* Detecting places where internal terminology has become self-referential.
* Reviewing a frozen package as an outside referee.

Observer independence should be deliberately preserved when useful.

13. Final Operating Principle

The objective is not to build an error-free research system. That is impossible.

The objective is to build a coupled system in which unsupported progress increasingly creates detectable inconsistencies elsewhere.

A mathematical claim should not be able to change materially without producing a traceable status, dependency, evidence, and architecture response.

An architectural mechanism should not be able to grow materially without demonstrating the uncertainty or failure risk it removes.
